WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.368
You’ve been driving it; now look under the hood.

2
00:00:02.648 --> 00:00:11.928
If you’ve used Claude Code for a year, you know what it feels like: you describe a problem, text scrolls past, files change, tests run, and a summary appears.

3
00:00:12.208 --> 00:00:15.109
This lesson slows that down so you can see the parts.

4
00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:22.728
Once you can name the parts, you can tell which one failed when a session goes wrong, and you can choose deliberately instead of hoping.

5
00:00:23.008 --> 00:00:26.635
Anthropic’s docs describe a coding agent as two components.

6
00:00:26.915 --> 00:00:31.480
The model reasons: it reads code, decides what to do next, and writes edits.

7
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:41.531
The harness is everything around the model: the tools it can call, the permission system that decides which calls run, and the machinery that manages what the model sees.

8
00:00:41.811 --> 00:00:44.477
Claude Code is a harness around Claude models.

9
00:00:44.757 --> 00:00:51.904
Codex, Gemini CLI, Copilot’s agents, and Cursor’s Agent are harnesses around their own model choices.

10
00:00:52.184 --> 00:01:04.749
Much of what distinguishes these products is harness design, which is why the same model can behave differently in two tools, and why a benchmark score for “model X” depends on which harness ran it (lesson 2).

11
00:01:05.029 --> 00:01:19.408
The loop the docs describe has three phases that blend together: gather context (search, read files, run commands to learn the state), take action (edit files, run commands), and verify results (run the tests, read the output).

12
00:01:19.688 --> 00:01:22.355
A question about your codebase might only gather.

13
00:01:22.635 --> 00:01:25.216
A bug fix cycles through all three many times.

14
00:01:25.496 --> 00:01:34.093
You can interrupt at any point, and that matters: the cheapest moment to correct a misunderstanding is in the first minute, before it has been built on.
